# Home-page one-liners - the rules, and where they came from

_This is the reference behind `../SKILL.md`, the one-screen checklist run on the one-line descriptions under entries on a course home page (`home.html`) before they reach Sathya. Read this file once at the start; run the checklist on the output. Every checklist line traces to a section here. When a rule changes here, change its test in SKILL.md in the same edit; when a test is added there, its rule and the correction it came from go here. Version history is at the end._

On a bare-bones course home page, each assignment, activity, or exam entry has a title and a single line underneath it: `You leave with: ...` for the current sprint, `You left with: ...` for past sprints, or a direct description for an exam or a closing story. Students scan the home page before opening an assignment page, so that one line is the only thing they read about the item.

The line has two jobs at once, and the design (29 Oct 2026) depends on both. For the current sprint, the line is a prompt: when the student writes their own closing sentence on the chapter page, that sentence replaces the line on the home page, in their words, with no label. For past sprints, nobody replaces it, so the line is the final word on what that item was for. In both cases the line must name something a student can check they have, because a student who did the item badly or late will read a claimed outcome ("an understanding of…") against themselves, and cannot replace it with anything of their own.

Sathya's own account of the three pulls on this line (29 Oct 2026): his academic training wants learning outcomes; he wants the line useful to the student at once, and more than an inventory, tied to their learning; and Claude's clipped noun phrases ("the path you chose, and why") read as Claude-speak students will not understand. The resolution: a learning outcome is assessable, and its assessable form is the thing the student can show, so the thing line and the outcome line are the same line when the thing is named precisely enough that the learning is in it, in a full sentence.

All before/after pairs below come from Sathya's 29 October 2026 revision of `cowork/fall-2026-courses/cst286-home-mockup-text.md` and the redraft that followed the same day.

## One thing the student has made

Name one concrete thing the student has made by the end of the item: the goal they wrote, the sources they judged, the prediction they explained. A student can look at a thing and check they have it. An outcome stated without its evidence ("an understanding of", "experience of", "having demonstrated") cannot be checked, and reads as a verdict on the student. A count of form boxes ("three scenarios · two look-alikes") is the thing with the learning removed.

- *My Sprint 2 experience, and the path I choose*
  - Claude's first draft: "You leave with: the path you chose, and why" (clipped)
  - Sathya's edit: "You leave with: an understanding of the three possible paths to explore during Sprint 3" (outcome, no evidence)
  - Redraft: "You leave with: your choice of a path for Sprint 3, and the reasons you gave for it"
- *Explore Physics Loop*
  - Claude's first draft: "You left with: one physics behavior you tinkered with and a question about it"
  - Sathya's edit: "You left with: experience of tinkering with physical system simulations"
  - Redraft: "You left with: a simulation of a physical system that you changed yourself, and what happened when you did"

## The learning is in the thing

The thing named includes the part that shows the student's thinking: the reasons they gave, the check they ran on themselves, the difference they named between two look-alikes, the habit they chose to change. This is where the metacognition lives; without it the line is an inventory.

- *Physics learning goal, and how you would demonstrate it*
  - Claude's first draft: "You left with: a physics goal, and what you would do to show you reached it"
  - Sathya's edit: "You left with: a physics learning goal, and how you verify your learning"
  - Redraft: "You left with: a physics learning goal, and the way you would check for yourself that you had reached it"
- *How well did I learn in Sprint 1*
  - Redraft: "You left with: your account of how you learned in Sprint 1, and the one habit you decided to change for Sprint 2"

## Full plain sentence

Write the thing out as a sentence a student reads once. A clipped noun phrase ("the path you chose, and why"; "the quantity you will predict, and from what") is Claude's habit and assumes the reader will unpack it; students will not. Longer than the clipped draft, shorter than the outcome version.

- *My Sprint 3 goal and plan*
  - Claude's first draft: "You leave with: the quantity you will predict, and from what"
  - Sathya's edit: "You leave with: a well-defined goal for learning about a physical system and its quantitative relationship" ("well-defined" is a grade, not a description)
  - Redraft: "You leave with: a goal that says which physical quantity you will be able to predict, and from what"

## Full course terms

Write out the course's concepts in full, self-contained terms (`a physical system and its quantitative relationship`, `the conditions it holds under`, `a physics learning goal`). Do not use shorthand lifted from inside the form (`from what` on its own, `the condition it needs`, `written as a prediction`) that a student can only decode after opening the assignment page.

- *Find and confirm my relationship*
  - Claude's first draft: "You leave with: the relationship, its units, and the condition it needs"
  - Sathya's edit: "You leave with: the quantitative relationship, its units, and the physical conditions in which it is applicable"
  - Redraft: "You leave with: the quantitative relationship you found, its units, the conditions it holds under, and how you confirmed it"

## Both halves

Where an item has two halves, name both: *how you learned* and *what you learned* in a sprint story; *closed-book written exam* and *partner conversation* in an exam.

- *My Sprint 3 experience*
  - Claude's first draft: "The story of what you learned, in your words"
  - Sathya's edit: "The story of how you learn and what you learned"
- *Sprint 3 Exam*
  - Claude's first draft: "In class, on paper"
  - Sathya's edit: "In class, closed-book written exam and partner conversation"

## No title echo or filler

The one-liner sits directly under the entry's title, so repeating the title's words (`your sources, and your judgement of each one` under `Find and judge your sources`) wastes the line. Use the line to add what the title leaves unsaid.

Cut voice filler and anti-AI tags (`actually`, `in your words`, `in your own words`, `your own read of`).

## The chain, without naming the next step

Each item's thing is what the next item starts from; that is how the home page reads as a chain. But the line names the thing itself, not the next item's use of it. Reducing an item to preparation for the exam or to a hand-off is what Sathya's first edit was rejecting, and the thing named well already carries the link.

- *Five real-world scenarios*
  - Claude's first draft: "You leave with: one scenario you can walk through on the exam" (names the next step)
  - Sathya's edit: "You leave with: having demonstrated where you can apply what you learned and where it doesn't apply" (outcome, no evidence)
  - Redraft: "You leave with: three real situations where your relationship predicts correctly, and two that look alike but where it does not, with the difference named"

